siso: support build outputs outside the exec root

When a build manifest named its outputs by absolute paths outside the
exec root, for example an Android OUT_DIR on a different disk than the
source tree, siso joined those absolute paths onto the exec root.
hashfs.Update stored every updated output under a name like
<exec root>/mnt/out/..., wrote that name into the journal, and tried to
set the mtime of a file that does not exist. updateDeps failed to make
the output relative to the working directory and returned before
recording anything, so the deps log stayed empty and was recompacted to
its 16-byte header on every start. The response file setup looked for
the file at the joined path, did not find it, and reported an unexpected
removal every time. Once deps were recorded, checkDeps refused every
compile whose depfile named a header generated in such a build
directory. The rule of the step set "remote" in the siso config, so the
step carried a platform, and checkDeps treated it as a remote request
that cannot hold an absolute path, even though no remote execution was
configured and the step ran locally.

Now such outputs are recorded under their own absolute path in the file
system state, in the journal and in the deps log. A local step may
depend on an absolute path outside the exec root: checkDeps checks that
it exists like any other dependency and the deps log keeps it, so a
change to a generated header rebuilds the steps that include it. A
second build with nothing changed does no work, and the deps log and the
recorded command hashes survive both a normal exit and a killed build.

This change routes the remaining exec root joins through helpers that
keep absolute paths as they are, which matches Path.FromWD: it already
keeps paths outside the exec root absolute. In hashfs, Update, the
symlink resolution in Entries and AddMissingOutput now use makeFullpath.
In the build package, updateDeps keys the deps log by the absolute
output path as written in the manifest, and the response file setup and
teardown, the reproxy output permission fix and the input tree for
non-local directories use a new absFromExecRoot helper. checkDeps keeps
refusing absolute dependencies on steps that siso may run remotely,
through remote execution, reproxy or a remote exec wrapper, as the new
Builder.mayRunRemote reports. The change adds a hashfs unit test, unit
tests of mayRunRemote and of that refusal, and two end-to-end tests
with a build directory outside the exec root, one of them with a remote
rule and a generated header. After a header change, the end-to-end
tests check which outputs were rebuilt, not only how many steps ran.
The nsjail sandbox is left as it is and still rejects absolute outputs.

An Android tree built with an absolute OUT_DIR outside the source tree
started every build from zero. The explain log blamed a missing deps
log entry for every compile step and a missing command line for many
other steps, and the siso log was full of paths with the exec root
prefixed to the absolute out directory. With only the path joins fixed,
the first build stopped early, with "absolute path in deps" on the
compiles that include generated headers.

Builds that keep the out directory on a separate disk become incremental
again, and interrupted builds keep the work they finished. Builds whose
build directory is inside the exec root behave as before, and builds
with remote execution keep the relocatability check.

Test: go test ./build/... ./hashfs/... ./execute/...
  ./toolsupport/ninjautil/... ./subcmd/ninja/... (all passed)
Test: new tests with the hashfs half reverted (the hashfs test and both
